1
00:00:00,233 --> 00:00:03,303
But what you could do with nuclear weapons

2
00:00:03,303 --> 00:00:06,639
is use a small number of them

3
00:00:07,440 --> 00:00:10,043
to convince the other side

4
00:00:10,043 --> 00:00:12,979
that you were deadly serious

5
00:00:12,979 --> 00:00:17,317
The West is leading
Ukraine down the primrose

6
00:00:17,317 --> 00:00:21,454
path, and the Israelis
are executing a genocide in Gaza.

7
00:00:21,521 --> 00:00:25,191
It's not China's fault
that it wants to be powerful.

8
00:00:25,225 --> 00:00:29,496
So if you get into trouble
and you dial 911,

9
00:00:29,496 --> 00:00:32,499
there's nobody at the other end. 

10
00:00:32,599 --> 00:00:33,533
Hi this is John Mearsheimer

11
00:00:33,533 --> 00:00:37,904
And what I'd like to talk about
today is nuclear weapons

12
00:00:37,904 --> 00:00:41,341
and in particular,
tactical nuclear weapons.

13
00:00:41,674 --> 00:00:45,111
There have been a lot of reports
in the press

14
00:00:45,412 --> 00:00:50,950
that the United States
is very interested in, quote unquote,

15
00:00:50,984 --> 00:00:56,656
expanding its options
when it comes to nuclear use.

16
00:00:57,023 --> 00:01:01,027
And in that context,
there has been much talk

17
00:01:01,194 --> 00:01:04,297
about relying more on tactical

18
00:01:04,297 --> 00:01:08,101
nuclear weapons
for purposes of deterrence

19
00:01:08,101 --> 00:01:12,105
and for purposes of war
fighting moving forward.

20
00:01:12,105 --> 00:01:17,911
And I'd like to talk about this issue

21
00:01:17,911 --> 00:01:20,947
of tactical nuclear weapons

22
00:01:21,014 --> 00:01:24,017
and what's going on here.

23
00:01:24,217 --> 00:01:28,455
What's driving this interest
in tactical nuclear weapons

24
00:01:28,455 --> 00:01:31,958
and whether or not it makes sense

25
00:01:32,425 --> 00:01:37,397
now with regard to what's driving
the interest in tactical nuclear weapons?

26
00:01:37,497 --> 00:01:42,068
I think, first of all,
there's no question that the United States

27
00:01:42,102 --> 00:01:48,541
is very interested in having many options

28
00:01:48,675 --> 00:01:52,612
when it comes to employing its nuclear
forces.

29
00:01:52,946 --> 00:01:56,483
You want to remember that
when the United States

30
00:01:56,649 --> 00:02:01,121
first developed its nuclear arsenal

31
00:02:01,221 --> 00:02:04,190
after World War Two,

32
00:02:04,190 --> 00:02:07,193
and especially in the 1950s,

33
00:02:07,193 --> 00:02:10,864
it employed a policy
of massive retaliation.

34
00:02:10,864 --> 00:02:15,635
And there was not
there were not a lot of options

35
00:02:15,969 --> 00:02:19,873
built in to the American

36
00:02:20,006 --> 00:02:23,710
doctrine for employing nuclear weapons.

37
00:02:23,743 --> 00:02:27,147
Again,
the emphasis was on massive retaliation,

38
00:02:27,614 --> 00:02:31,017
not flexible response or flexible options.

39
00:02:31,017 --> 00:02:34,053
But as time went by,

40
00:02:34,053 --> 00:02:37,123
certainly starting in the late

41
00:02:37,157 --> 00:02:42,695
1950s, early 1960s, we began to think
long and hard about just having

42
00:02:42,695 --> 00:02:48,301
a lot more flexibility in terms
of how we use those nuclear weapons.

43
00:02:48,334 --> 00:02:51,471
So I find it hardly surprising that

44
00:02:51,471 --> 00:02:54,474
the United States is interested

45
00:02:54,474 --> 00:02:59,979
in expanding its options
from that perspective.

46
00:02:59,979 --> 00:03:04,551
But I actually believe
that's not the main force driver here.

47
00:03:04,817 --> 00:03:09,355
I believe what really matters
is that we have discovered,

48
00:03:10,390 --> 00:03:11,457
as a consequence of

49
00:03:11,457 --> 00:03:15,028
the Ukraine war,
and especially the Iran war,

50
00:03:15,395 --> 00:03:20,633
that our conventional forces
are much weaker

51
00:03:20,934 --> 00:03:24,704
relative to our principal adversaries

52
00:03:24,704 --> 00:03:29,142
than we recognized a few years ago.

53
00:03:29,242 --> 00:03:33,513
I think what happened here

54
00:03:33,580 --> 00:03:37,083
is that during the unipolar moment,

55
00:03:37,150 --> 00:03:40,153
the United States

56
00:03:40,587 --> 00:03:43,256
basically allowed its industrial

57
00:03:43,256 --> 00:03:46,259
base to wither away.

58
00:03:46,259 --> 00:03:49,395
And we discovered

59
00:03:49,395 --> 00:03:54,067
in the Ukraine war
that if you get involved in a conflict

60
00:03:54,067 --> 00:03:57,437
with a country like Russia,
and this is true of China as well,

61
00:03:58,037 --> 00:04:01,040
you need a robust industrial base

62
00:04:01,040 --> 00:04:04,043
to produce the weaponry to wage that war.

63
00:04:04,444 --> 00:04:08,481
One of the advantages that the Russians
have over the Ukrainians,

64
00:04:08,481 --> 00:04:12,252
and even over the Europeans
and the Americans, is that the Russians

65
00:04:12,252 --> 00:04:17,423
have a robust industrial base
so they can produce weaponry

66
00:04:17,457 --> 00:04:22,161
over the long term for purposes
of waging a protracted conflict.

67
00:04:22,362 --> 00:04:26,933
If the United States
were to get involved in a war with China.

68
00:04:27,066 --> 00:04:30,503
China has a robust industrial base
and it can continue.

69
00:04:30,536 --> 00:04:33,506
It could continue that war for years
on end.

70
00:04:33,506 --> 00:04:37,877
The United States would be in deep trouble
after a few months because, again,

71
00:04:37,910 --> 00:04:42,415
it does not have 
a sufficient industrial base

72
00:04:42,415 --> 00:04:45,652
to wage a protracted conventional war.

73
00:04:46,252 --> 00:04:51,224
And then, of course,
if you go to the Iran war, it's very clear

74
00:04:51,224 --> 00:04:54,861
that we have run down our stockpile

75
00:04:54,861 --> 00:04:58,631
of precious, precious munitions
in that conflict.

76
00:04:59,065 --> 00:05:03,936
And the end result
is that our conventional deterrence

77
00:05:03,936 --> 00:05:07,373
has been has been weakened.

78
00:05:07,540 --> 00:05:12,312
You always want to remember that
if you're the United States or any other

79
00:05:12,578 --> 00:05:17,950
great power and you're thinking
about deterring an adversary,

80
00:05:17,984 --> 00:05:23,456
you rely on both conventional deterrence
and nuclear deterrence,

81
00:05:23,923 --> 00:05:27,894
and you pay careful attention
to what the balance is

82
00:05:27,894 --> 00:05:32,465
between conventional deterrence
and nuclear deterrence.

83
00:05:32,465 --> 00:05:35,802
And I'll talk more about that
as we go along.

84
00:05:35,802 --> 00:05:41,307
But we're in a situation now, the United
States, where our conventional forces

85
00:05:41,374 --> 00:05:44,444
have been greatly weakened

86
00:05:44,744 --> 00:05:48,014
as a result of the Iran war

87
00:05:48,014 --> 00:05:52,952
and as a result of supplying
the Ukrainians with weaponry.

88
00:05:52,952 --> 00:05:56,389
And furthermore, as I said,
we also realized that we do not have

89
00:05:56,389 --> 00:06:00,593
a robust industrial base,
which would put us at a disadvantage

90
00:06:00,827 --> 00:06:04,230
in a protracted conventional war.

91
00:06:05,098 --> 00:06:07,800
So, given

92
00:06:07,800 --> 00:06:11,804
that our conventional deterrence
has been weakened,

93
00:06:12,038 --> 00:06:15,675
it's hardly surprising
that people are now beginning

94
00:06:15,708 --> 00:06:18,711
to pay more attention
to nuclear deterrence,

95
00:06:18,745 --> 00:06:24,384
because that's the way this works 
and what's going on.

96
00:06:24,684 --> 00:06:28,721
Apparently,
is that a lot of people seem to think

97
00:06:28,855 --> 00:06:34,060
that if we emphasize nuclear deterrence,
we should pay careful attention.

98
00:06:34,060 --> 00:06:38,998
Or maybe special attention is a better way
to put it to tactical nuclear weapons.

99
00:06:39,298 --> 00:06:44,003
The tactical nuclear weapons
are well suited

100
00:06:44,137 --> 00:06:47,140
for use on the battlefield.

101
00:06:47,306 --> 00:06:50,309
If you get involved in a war,

102
00:06:50,643 --> 00:06:54,313
whether it's in Europe or in East Asia,

103
00:06:54,313 --> 00:06:58,284
and you don't feel that you have
sufficient conventional forces,

104
00:06:58,317 --> 00:07:04,524
you can fight that war
with with tactical nuclear weapons.

105
00:07:04,991 --> 00:07:07,093
I don't think this is true.

106
00:07:07,093 --> 00:07:10,963
I think this kind of thinking
is misguided.

107
00:07:10,963 --> 00:07:15,635
And I'd like to make the case for why
I think that's the case.

108
00:07:15,802 --> 00:07:21,107
And the best way to do
it is to go back to the 1950s,

109
00:07:21,574 --> 00:07:25,111
which is a period where we emphasized
employing

110
00:07:25,144 --> 00:07:30,750
tactical nuclear weapons
and talk about our experience

111
00:07:30,750 --> 00:07:35,621
in the 1950s,
and to focus laser like on a particular

112
00:07:37,256 --> 00:07:39,225
operation that took place.

113
00:07:39,225 --> 00:07:42,228
This was a war game called Carte Blanche

114
00:07:42,228 --> 00:07:47,333
that took place in June of 1955.

115
00:07:47,333 --> 00:07:50,403
It ran from June 20th, 1955

116
00:07:50,470 --> 00:07:53,439
to June 28th, 1955.

117
00:07:53,439 --> 00:07:55,708
Talk about Carte Bosch.

118
00:07:55,708 --> 00:08:00,413
But before I get to carte blanche,
I just want to paint a broad stroke

119
00:08:00,413 --> 00:08:04,383
picture of how we thought about deterrence

120
00:08:04,383 --> 00:08:07,620
in Europe in the 1950s.

121
00:08:07,753 --> 00:08:12,058
The United States and its allies,
of course, were interested

122
00:08:12,124 --> 00:08:15,661
in deterring the Soviet Union.

123
00:08:16,362 --> 00:08:20,266
And when you talk about deterrence,

124
00:08:20,266 --> 00:08:24,036
it's important to understand
that you also have to be concerned

125
00:08:24,070 --> 00:08:29,075
with warfighting,
because deterrence might not work.

126
00:08:29,509 --> 00:08:31,043
Deterrence might fail.

127
00:08:31,043 --> 00:08:34,013
And if deterrence fails,
then you have to fight a war.

128
00:08:34,146 --> 00:08:39,719
So the United States throughout the 1950s
thought long and hard

129
00:08:39,752 --> 00:08:45,258
about had deter the Soviets
from attacking into Western Europe.

130
00:08:45,324 --> 00:08:48,294
And should deterrence fail.

131
00:08:48,361 --> 00:08:51,797
How to fight a war
against the Soviet Union.

132
00:08:51,831 --> 00:08:56,836
Now, the problem that the Americans
faced from their perspective

133
00:08:56,936 --> 00:09:02,441
was that the Soviet Union had a formidable
conventional army.

134
00:09:02,675 --> 00:09:05,845
Remember,
the Soviet Union played the principal role

135
00:09:05,845 --> 00:09:09,482
in defeating the Wehrmacht
in World War Two.

136
00:09:09,615 --> 00:09:14,020
So throughout the 1950s, most Western

137
00:09:14,053 --> 00:09:20,560
defense experts or Western policymakers
viewed the Soviet military

138
00:09:20,560 --> 00:09:24,564
and especially the Soviet army,
as a formidable force.

139
00:09:24,997 --> 00:09:29,835
Furthermore, it's very important
to understand that West Germany,

140
00:09:29,835 --> 00:09:33,839
which eventually became a key player

141
00:09:33,839 --> 00:09:39,312
in the NATO
alliance, did not become a member of NATO

142
00:09:39,345 --> 00:09:43,082
until 1955, and it did not

143
00:09:43,082 --> 00:09:46,652
begin rearming until 1955.

144
00:09:46,719 --> 00:09:51,724
So from the period from 1950 to 1955.

145
00:09:51,824 --> 00:09:54,827
There was in effect no German army.

146
00:09:54,894 --> 00:09:57,863
Furthermore, the French were busy fighting

147
00:09:57,863 --> 00:10:00,866
against the Vietnamese in Indochina.

148
00:10:00,900 --> 00:10:04,136
You remember the famous
battle of the NBN Phu,

149
00:10:04,203 --> 00:10:08,441
which put an end to the French position

150
00:10:08,441 --> 00:10:11,477
in Vietnam, took place in 1954.

151
00:10:11,510 --> 00:10:14,747
So France was distracted
in a very important way.

152
00:10:14,747 --> 00:10:19,118
There was no German military
of any consequence,

153
00:10:19,185 --> 00:10:22,922
and the United States had limited forces

154
00:10:22,922 --> 00:10:26,392
in Europe, limited conventional forces.

155
00:10:26,492 --> 00:10:30,596
So what we decided to do in the 1950s

156
00:10:31,197 --> 00:10:35,201
was to deploy large numbers

157
00:10:35,201 --> 00:10:40,673
of tactical nuclear weapons
to Europe and rely

158
00:10:40,740 --> 00:10:45,978
heavily on tactical nuclear weapons,
not only for deterrence purposes,

159
00:10:46,779 --> 00:10:50,316
not only to deter
a Soviet conventional attack,

160
00:10:50,316 --> 00:10:54,220
but for war fighting purposes as well.

161
00:10:54,320 --> 00:10:57,123
And by the way,
this is why the United States

162
00:10:57,123 --> 00:11:00,693
has never had a no first use policy.

163
00:11:00,760 --> 00:11:02,028
The United States has never had a

164
00:11:02,028 --> 00:11:05,598
no first use policy,
because it is always understood

165
00:11:05,798 --> 00:11:11,737
that it might have to use nuclear weapons
to rescue the situation.

166
00:11:11,737 --> 00:11:16,609
If it was losing a conventional war
against the Soviet Union.

167
00:11:16,642 --> 00:11:21,714
So in the 1950s,
when NATO conventional forces

168
00:11:21,714 --> 00:11:24,817
were not especially strong
and the Soviet Union

169
00:11:24,817 --> 00:11:27,920
presented a formidable threat.

170
00:11:27,987 --> 00:11:30,990
We believed that

171
00:11:30,990 --> 00:11:35,628
if deterrence failed
and the Soviets were to attack,

172
00:11:35,661 --> 00:11:38,631
we would have to turn to nuclear weapons

173
00:11:38,631 --> 00:11:42,134
very quickly, if not immediately.

174
00:11:42,234 --> 00:11:47,740
So it's just very important to understand
that tactical nuclear weapons

175
00:11:47,740 --> 00:11:53,879
were seeing for much of the 1950s
as a magic weapon.

176
00:11:54,613 --> 00:11:57,483
Now, what happened to throw

177
00:11:57,483 --> 00:12:01,320
cold water on this whole line of thinking

178
00:12:01,320 --> 00:12:04,790
was a war game that was run

179
00:12:04,824 --> 00:12:07,827
in mid June 1955.

180
00:12:07,860 --> 00:12:13,099
This is called carte blanche, and

181
00:12:13,099 --> 00:12:18,337
we had the situation where the Soviets
would attack with conventional forces.

182
00:12:18,404 --> 00:12:23,976
We would use nuclear weapons,
tactical nuclear weapons in response.

183
00:12:24,043 --> 00:12:30,116
And of course, the Soviets
would use nuclear weapons of their own.

184
00:12:30,316 --> 00:12:33,319
Now, what happened in this war game,

185
00:12:33,919 --> 00:12:39,658
basically about 3000

186
00:12:39,892 --> 00:12:44,230
NATO aircraft
from 11 different countries were employed

187
00:12:44,697 --> 00:12:47,867
in the war game. And

188
00:12:48,901 --> 00:12:51,036
about,

189
00:12:51,036 --> 00:12:54,173
I'd say, 335

190
00:12:54,340 --> 00:12:57,343
nuclear weapons were employed.

191
00:12:57,643 --> 00:13:01,714
The usual range is 335 to 355.

192
00:13:01,747 --> 00:13:08,220
That's how many tactical nuclear weapons
were employed in this war game.

193
00:13:08,521 --> 00:13:13,759
And by the way,
the Russians, of course, employed

194
00:13:13,893 --> 00:13:18,964
nuclear weapons as well as the NATO forces
in this war game.

195
00:13:18,998 --> 00:13:23,502
And in fact,
the Russians or the Soviets ended up

196
00:13:23,736 --> 00:13:26,739
using or deploying or

197
00:13:26,772 --> 00:13:32,344
exploding more nuclear weapons, slightly
more nuclear weapons than Naito did.

198
00:13:32,344 --> 00:13:36,916
But let's say the total was about 335
nuclear weapons.

199
00:13:36,916 --> 00:13:42,121
And the war was fought
almost exclusively on West German soil.

200
00:13:42,454 --> 00:13:44,590
Now, what was the end result?

201
00:13:44,590 --> 00:13:47,726
The end result is that 1.7

202
00:13:47,726 --> 00:13:51,463
million Germans died, West Germans

203
00:13:51,463 --> 00:13:55,434
died, and 3.5 million

204
00:13:55,601 --> 00:13:58,704
West Germans were wounded.

205
00:14:00,139 --> 00:14:04,310
Moreover,
these numbers don't take into account

206
00:14:04,343 --> 00:14:10,916
the subsequent deaths that would occur
as a result of the radiation poisoning.

207
00:14:10,983 --> 00:14:12,852
So you just want to think about that.

208
00:14:12,852 --> 00:14:17,923
You had a war game
that went from June 20th to June 28th.

209
00:14:18,057 --> 00:14:21,360
This is 1955, the middle of the 50s,

210
00:14:21,393 --> 00:14:25,764
and it ended up with 1.7

211
00:14:25,998 --> 00:14:29,835
1.7 million West Germans dead.

212
00:14:30,936 --> 00:14:32,605
What is this tell you?

213
00:14:32,605 --> 00:14:37,509
In effect,
it tells you that you're destroying

214
00:14:37,509 --> 00:14:40,512
Germany to save Germany.

215
00:14:41,547 --> 00:14:44,717
This operation,

216
00:14:44,717 --> 00:14:48,520
this war game, was viewed

217
00:14:48,520 --> 00:14:53,859
as evidence that it was hard to see how

218
00:14:54,493 --> 00:14:57,229
you could defend West

219
00:14:57,229 --> 00:15:00,399
Germany with tactical nuclear weapons

220
00:15:00,399 --> 00:15:04,303
without destroying it
as a functioning country.

221
00:15:04,536 --> 00:15:07,306
And it raised all sorts of

222
00:15:07,306 --> 00:15:10,309
concerns, unsurprisingly,

223
00:15:10,376 --> 00:15:14,947
in West Germany,
about whether this was a viable strategy

224
00:15:14,947 --> 00:15:18,284
for defending Germany

225
00:15:18,317 --> 00:15:23,355
or defending Western Europe
against a possible Soviet attack.

226
00:15:23,589 --> 00:15:28,994
When people calculated how much
destruction had resulted from this war

227
00:15:29,028 --> 00:15:34,199
game, it became clear to many people
that given this level of destruction,

228
00:15:34,233 --> 00:15:37,703
tactical
nuclear weapons did not make much sense.

229
00:15:38,237 --> 00:15:40,973
There was another dimension

230
00:15:40,973 --> 00:15:44,076
that came into play here
that really mattered.

231
00:15:44,310 --> 00:15:46,946
And that's the issue of escalation.

232
00:15:46,946 --> 00:15:48,614
In the war game itself.

233
00:15:48,614 --> 00:15:54,553
It became very clear that it was hard
to control escalation. And

234
00:15:56,355 --> 00:15:59,425
there is no question that

235
00:15:59,792 --> 00:16:02,895
it's hard to draw a clear line

236
00:16:02,895 --> 00:16:08,133
between tactical nuclear weapons,
intermediate range

237
00:16:08,133 --> 00:16:11,937
nuclear weapons
and strategic nuclear weapons.

238
00:16:12,104 --> 00:16:15,374
And not only is it hard to draw

239
00:16:15,374 --> 00:16:19,578
that line, it's a slippery slope
so that once

240
00:16:19,578 --> 00:16:23,582
you start using tactical nuclear weapons,
you quickly

241
00:16:23,615 --> 00:16:27,920
find yourself in a situation
where you're thinking about using

242
00:16:28,587 --> 00:16:33,292
intermediate range nuclear weapons
or strategic nuclear weapons.

243
00:16:34,059 --> 00:16:39,431
On top of that, because we
thankfully have never had a nuclear war.

244
00:16:39,498 --> 00:16:43,068
We have very little understanding

245
00:16:43,102 --> 00:16:47,106
of the dynamics of escalation
in a nuclear war.

246
00:16:47,106 --> 00:16:52,244
And you want to understand that
in a nuclear war, if you're interested in

247
00:16:52,244 --> 00:16:58,117
limiting it to West Germany here,
we're talking about the NATO scenario.

248
00:16:58,150 --> 00:17:00,152
You need excellent command and control.

249
00:17:00,152 --> 00:17:04,623
And that's true in any nuclear war, right?

250
00:17:05,324 --> 00:17:08,560
If you want to keep it limited,
you have to have excellent command

251
00:17:08,560 --> 00:17:10,062
and control systems.

252
00:17:10,062 --> 00:17:14,466
And there's all sorts
of evidence that it'll be

253
00:17:15,534 --> 00:17:16,735
very difficult,

254
00:17:16,735 --> 00:17:20,639
if not impossible, to maintain
meaningful command and control

255
00:17:20,639 --> 00:17:24,443
as you go up the escalation ladder
in a nuclear conflict.

256
00:17:24,743 --> 00:17:28,414
So what happens after carte blanche

257
00:17:28,580 --> 00:17:35,220
is we come to realize
that tactical nuclear weapons

258
00:17:35,254 --> 00:17:40,826
really are not a viable way
of defending West Germany

259
00:17:40,826 --> 00:17:44,863
because of the destruction
that would be wrought on that country.

260
00:17:44,863 --> 00:17:48,734
And furthermore,
we come to quickly understand

261
00:17:49,068 --> 00:17:52,638
that the threat of escalation is

262
00:17:53,672 --> 00:17:59,044
so great that you can't allow yourself

263
00:17:59,078 --> 00:18:05,517
to think that you can fight
a limited nuclear war of the sort

264
00:18:05,551 --> 00:18:10,055
that played out in carte blanche
and keep it limited.

265
00:18:10,055 --> 00:18:14,359
And indeed, there is a serious possibility

266
00:18:14,359 --> 00:18:20,632
that if you were in real life
to engage in a war

267
00:18:20,732 --> 00:18:23,735
in West Germany and

268
00:18:24,470 --> 00:18:28,740
the NATO forces
and the Soviet forces of the Warsaw

269
00:18:28,774 --> 00:18:33,412
Pact forces were to use
significant numbers of nuclear weapons,

270
00:18:33,412 --> 00:18:36,782
it wasn't clear that you could keep it
limited.

271
00:18:37,349 --> 00:18:39,685
In fact, there was
there were all sorts of reasons

272
00:18:39,685 --> 00:18:42,855
to think it would escalate
to a general thermonuclear war, which,

273
00:18:42,921 --> 00:18:47,159
of course, nobody wanted anything

274
00:18:47,860 --> 00:18:50,329
to go back to the 50s.

275
00:18:50,329 --> 00:18:55,000
What happens is that in 1957,

276
00:18:55,067 --> 00:18:58,203
amazingly, two years after

277
00:18:59,238 --> 00:19:02,641
Carte Blanche, NATO formalizes

278
00:19:03,675 --> 00:19:05,043
a military doctrine.

279
00:19:05,043 --> 00:19:08,614
It's called MC 14 two.

280
00:19:09,148 --> 00:19:12,351
NATO formalizes a military doctrine

281
00:19:12,551 --> 00:19:18,457
which calls for mass abuse
of nuclear weapons to defend Europe,

282
00:19:18,457 --> 00:19:23,962
and places a great emphasis
on using tactical nuclear weapons.

283
00:19:23,962 --> 00:19:27,900
That's MC 14 two and that's

284
00:19:28,534 --> 00:19:32,337
that's approved by NATO in the spring

285
00:19:32,337 --> 00:19:36,108
of 1957, two years after Carte Blanche.

286
00:19:37,276 --> 00:19:41,280
But then what begins to happen over time

287
00:19:41,947 --> 00:19:46,652
is that NATO begins to rethink this issue,

288
00:19:46,818 --> 00:19:51,156
given what happened with carte blanche,
and given all the studies

289
00:19:51,156 --> 00:19:54,893
that are subsequently done
on the subject of escalation

290
00:19:55,060 --> 00:19:59,665
and nuclear flexibility,

291
00:20:00,199 --> 00:20:03,835
we begin to realize that.

292
00:20:03,835 --> 00:20:08,907
A doctrine for defending Europe

293
00:20:08,907 --> 00:20:12,177
that relies on tactical nuclear weapons

294
00:20:12,477 --> 00:20:15,314
is not a good idea.

295
00:20:15,314 --> 00:20:17,549
In fact, it's a very bad idea.

296
00:20:17,549 --> 00:20:20,552
So two things happen here.

297
00:20:20,752 --> 00:20:25,023
The first is that NATO decides

298
00:20:25,224 --> 00:20:29,461
that it's going to emphasize
conventional deterrence

299
00:20:29,494 --> 00:20:35,534
much more in the future
than it had in the past.

300
00:20:35,701 --> 00:20:39,705
Remember
I said before that if you're a great power

301
00:20:39,705 --> 00:20:42,641
and you're thinking about how to deter
an adversary,

302
00:20:42,641 --> 00:20:44,142
you have to think about the balance

303
00:20:44,142 --> 00:20:47,212
between conventional deterrence
and nuclear deterrence.

304
00:20:47,212 --> 00:20:50,015
How much do you want to rely on
conventional deterrence?

305
00:20:50,015 --> 00:20:52,517
How much do you want
to rely on nuclear deterrence?

306
00:20:52,517 --> 00:20:56,388
There's no question, given that we live
in a nuclear world, that nuclear

307
00:20:56,388 --> 00:21:01,193
deterrence and conventional deterrence
are both going to be present all the time.

308
00:21:01,193 --> 00:21:04,896
But the question is, what is the balance
look like between the two?

309
00:21:05,264 --> 00:21:08,834
And what I'm telling you
is that during the 1950,

310
00:21:08,867 --> 00:21:14,740
the balance was heavily skewed in favor
of nuclear deterrence in Europe.

311
00:21:14,906 --> 00:21:17,909
And this, of course, is

312
00:21:18,043 --> 00:21:22,814
seen in MC 14 two,
which is the NATO doctrine

313
00:21:22,814 --> 00:21:28,820
that's put into place
in the spring of 1957.

314
00:21:28,820 --> 00:21:35,160
But what happens over time
is that NATO de-emphasize

315
00:21:35,160 --> 00:21:40,532
its reliance on nuclear deterrence,
and it begins to build formidable

316
00:21:40,532 --> 00:21:45,037
conventional forces,
and it begins to rely much more

317
00:21:45,304 --> 00:21:48,273
on conventional deterrence.

318
00:21:48,273 --> 00:21:52,444
And this is possible in good part

319
00:21:52,577 --> 00:21:55,747
because the German army

320
00:21:55,747 --> 00:21:59,184
comes online after 1955.

321
00:21:59,217 --> 00:22:04,056
Remember, Germany joins NATO in 1955

322
00:22:04,056 --> 00:22:10,762
and Germany rearmed in 1955,
where it starts rearmament in 1955.

323
00:22:10,796 --> 00:22:15,067
This is the same year, same time
frame as carte blanche.

324
00:22:15,067 --> 00:22:18,804
And what happens over the course
of the second half

325
00:22:18,804 --> 00:22:23,241
of the 1950s, in the early
1960s, is the Germans develop

326
00:22:23,241 --> 00:22:28,480
a very formidable fighting force, 
conventional fighting for us.

327
00:22:28,480 --> 00:22:31,383
Remember, the Germans
did not have nuclear weapons at the time.

328
00:22:31,383 --> 00:22:33,852
The Germans, of course,
still have no nuclear weapons.

329
00:22:33,852 --> 00:22:38,390
But the Germans had a very formidable
conventional army.

330
00:22:38,390 --> 00:22:42,828
And the United States commits to building

331
00:22:42,828 --> 00:22:47,632
more powerful conventional forces
in Europe as well.

332
00:22:47,632 --> 00:22:53,338
And we begin to de-emphasize
nuclear deterrence, which is another way

333
00:22:53,338 --> 00:22:59,378
of saying we begin to de-emphasize
our emphasis on tactical nuclear weapons.

334
00:22:59,378 --> 00:23:02,914
And we rely much more on
conventional deterrence.

335
00:23:03,215 --> 00:23:06,785
Now, the second thing that we do

336
00:23:06,785 --> 00:23:09,788
is that we fully understand

337
00:23:09,821 --> 00:23:14,593
that we want to continue
to rely on nuclear deterrence.

338
00:23:15,060 --> 00:23:20,265
If conventional deterrence
and nuclear deterrence fail

339
00:23:20,665 --> 00:23:25,670
to stop the Soviets from attacking
and the Soviets attack,

340
00:23:26,438 --> 00:23:30,075
we will first try to stop them
conventionally.

341
00:23:30,142 --> 00:23:33,311
This is what happens in the 1960s.

342
00:23:33,545 --> 00:23:39,518
We decide by then that if the Soviets
attack into Western Europe,

343
00:23:39,518 --> 00:23:44,589
we will first try to stop them
with the formidable conventional forces

344
00:23:44,589 --> 00:23:50,495
that NATO built up in the late 50s
and in the early 1960s.

345
00:23:50,796 --> 00:23:55,066
And you want to remember that
back in the mid 50s,

346
00:23:55,133 --> 00:23:59,104
at the time of court, Blanche,
if the Soviets were to attack,

347
00:23:59,137 --> 00:24:04,676
we would almost automatically
go to using tactical nuclear weapons.

348
00:24:04,676 --> 00:24:07,679
There's
no real conventional deterrence there.

349
00:24:08,180 --> 00:24:09,748
For warfighting purposes.

350
00:24:09,748 --> 00:24:12,317
We were going to rely on
tactical nuclear weapons.

351
00:24:12,317 --> 00:24:16,021
That all changes by the early 1960s.

352
00:24:16,021 --> 00:24:19,124
And of course, we're relying
on conventional deterrence.

353
00:24:19,124 --> 00:24:23,628
But everybody fully understands
that there's a possibility

354
00:24:23,628 --> 00:24:27,265
that the conventional forces will fail

355
00:24:27,833 --> 00:24:31,870
to stop the Soviets
and the Soviets and their allies,

356
00:24:31,870 --> 00:24:36,575
and the Warsaw Pact
will overrun Europe, Western Europe.

357
00:24:36,575 --> 00:24:41,480
So the question that NATO then faces is,
how should it

358
00:24:41,480 --> 00:24:45,283
think about using its nuclear weapons?

359
00:24:45,550 --> 00:24:50,255
Now everybody remembers
what happened with carte blanche, right?

360
00:24:50,322 --> 00:24:54,259
So there's no incentive

361
00:24:54,292 --> 00:24:57,295
to fight a conventional war.

362
00:24:58,063 --> 00:24:59,164
Let me put it differently.

363
00:24:59,164 --> 00:25:02,467
To fight a war, a failed conventional war

364
00:25:02,467 --> 00:25:05,871
with tactical nuclear weapons
in West Germany.

365
00:25:05,871 --> 00:25:11,977
So there's no incentive to massive use
of tactical nuclear weapons.

366
00:25:11,977 --> 00:25:15,814
Should the conventional forces
of NATO fail

367
00:25:15,814 --> 00:25:19,317
to stop the Warsaw Pact
conventional forces.

368
00:25:19,484 --> 00:25:24,656
So what NATO does is
come up with a clever doctrine.

369
00:25:25,724 --> 00:25:29,261
It says that should

370
00:25:29,594 --> 00:25:34,065
the Warsaw Pact begin to overrun NATO's

371
00:25:34,065 --> 00:25:38,203
conventional forces, what NATO will do is

372
00:25:38,403 --> 00:25:43,141
it will deploy a handful

373
00:25:43,141 --> 00:25:49,114
of nuclear weapons against targets,
maybe in East Germany,

374
00:25:49,147 --> 00:25:54,519
maybe in Czechoslovakia, maybe in Poland,
just a handful of nuclear weapons

375
00:25:54,586 --> 00:25:59,925
to convey to the Soviet Union
that it was deadly

376
00:25:59,925 --> 00:26:03,395
serious about stopping the war

377
00:26:03,395 --> 00:26:09,834
and preventing the Warsaw Pact
from overrunning all of Europe.

378
00:26:09,868 --> 00:26:13,471
In other words, what NATO planned to do

379
00:26:13,505 --> 00:26:18,310
was to launch
a very limited nuclear attack

380
00:26:18,577 --> 00:26:24,716
for throwing both sides
at on the slippery slope to oblivion.

381
00:26:25,984 --> 00:26:29,821
NATO was not interested
in launching a handful of nuclear weapons

382
00:26:29,821 --> 00:26:33,825
to win the war in any military sense,

383
00:26:33,959 --> 00:26:36,828
NATO had figured out,

384
00:26:36,828 --> 00:26:39,030
as a result of carte blanche

385
00:26:39,030 --> 00:26:42,200
and the subsequent analysis that was done,

386
00:26:42,200 --> 00:26:46,404
that you could not win a war
with nuclear weapons.

387
00:26:46,972 --> 00:26:49,441
But what you could do with nuclear

388
00:26:49,441 --> 00:26:53,378
weapons is use a small number of them

389
00:26:54,179 --> 00:26:59,284
to convince the other side
that you were deadly serious

390
00:26:59,284 --> 00:27:04,222
about coercing them to stop their attack,

391
00:27:04,356 --> 00:27:10,562
and it was really the threat of escalation
that was at the heart of this strategy.

392
00:27:10,562 --> 00:27:15,700
In other words, the idea was that if
you used the handful of nuclear weapons,

393
00:27:16,267 --> 00:27:18,903
there would be an ever present threat

394
00:27:18,903 --> 00:27:22,440
of further escalation
if the Soviets didn't

395
00:27:22,440 --> 00:27:25,977
stop their conventional offensive
against Western Europe,

396
00:27:26,244 --> 00:27:29,781
the United States would use
maybe a few more nuclear weapons.

397
00:27:29,781 --> 00:27:34,052
And who knows where this would lead.

398
00:27:34,419 --> 00:27:38,990
So you see that what happens is

399
00:27:38,990 --> 00:27:44,229
that it's not the tactical nuclear weapons
or small nuclear weapons disappear.

400
00:27:45,296 --> 00:27:48,299
What happens is that NATO

401
00:27:48,733 --> 00:27:52,404
thinks about using those nuclear weapons

402
00:27:52,437 --> 00:27:56,107
in a much more limited way, in a way

403
00:27:56,107 --> 00:28:00,045
that has really nothing to do with war
fighting.

404
00:28:00,278 --> 00:28:02,881
MC 14 slash two.

405
00:28:02,881 --> 00:28:07,052
This is the 1957 version

406
00:28:07,352 --> 00:28:10,822
of NATO strategy
on the employment of nuclear weapons.

407
00:28:11,256 --> 00:28:14,025
MC 14 two basically calls

408
00:28:14,025 --> 00:28:17,028
for war
fighting with tactical nuclear weapons.

409
00:28:17,262 --> 00:28:22,534
If you look at MC 14 three,

410
00:28:24,002 --> 00:28:26,104
which is adopted by NATO

411
00:28:26,104 --> 00:28:30,041
in 1967, ten years later,

412
00:28:31,242 --> 00:28:35,413
it calls for using nuclear weapons

413
00:28:35,413 --> 00:28:38,516
in a very limited way

414
00:28:38,950 --> 00:28:43,188
for purposes of basically
putting both sides out

415
00:28:43,221 --> 00:28:48,893
on the slippery slope to oblivion
and convincing the Soviets

416
00:28:48,893 --> 00:28:55,500
or the Warsaw Pact in this case,
that it should stop its offensive

417
00:28:55,533 --> 00:29:01,072
against Western Europe,
because the United States will increase

418
00:29:01,072 --> 00:29:04,843
the number of its attacks
with nuclear weapons

419
00:29:04,843 --> 00:29:10,115
if the Soviets don't stop, and
at some point this could escalate into a

420
00:29:10,615 --> 00:29:13,618
a general thermonuclear war
that the Soviet

421
00:29:13,618 --> 00:29:16,654
Union certainly does not want.

422
00:29:16,721 --> 00:29:20,592
Now, there are many people
who think that NATO strategy,

423
00:29:21,059 --> 00:29:24,462
as laid out in MC 14

424
00:29:24,863 --> 00:29:27,732
three, makes no sense.

425
00:29:27,732 --> 00:29:30,735
That's an issue for another day.

426
00:29:30,935 --> 00:29:36,775
The point that I'm trying to make here
is that the United States of America,

427
00:29:36,841 --> 00:29:41,079
once upon a time, placed a great emphasis

428
00:29:41,179 --> 00:29:44,082
on nuclear deterrence

429
00:29:44,082 --> 00:29:48,453
and nuclear war
fighting with tactical nuclear weapons.

430
00:29:48,920 --> 00:29:52,390
And the logic that was employed in

431
00:29:52,423 --> 00:29:56,895
that case had to do with the fact
that we did not feel that

432
00:29:56,895 --> 00:30:02,700
we had sufficient conventional forces
to deter or fight the Soviet Union.

433
00:30:03,101 --> 00:30:06,671
We didn't have sufficient nuclear
excuse me.

434
00:30:06,704 --> 00:30:07,705
We didn't have sufficient

435
00:30:07,705 --> 00:30:12,510
conventional deterrence, and therefore
we had to rely on nuclear deterrence,

436
00:30:12,510 --> 00:30:17,215
which in effect meant employing
tactical nuclear weapons.

437
00:30:17,215 --> 00:30:21,586
But it quickly became clear
with carte blanche

438
00:30:21,920 --> 00:30:25,857
that was simply not a viable strategy.

439
00:30:25,857 --> 00:30:27,959
And we moved away from it.

440
00:30:27,959 --> 00:30:31,429
And again,
we moved away from it in two ways.

441
00:30:31,429 --> 00:30:34,599
And by the way,
those two ways all were placed

442
00:30:34,599 --> 00:30:38,236
under the umbrella
of flexible response, right?

443
00:30:38,903 --> 00:30:42,774
The first way we moved away from
it was we increased

444
00:30:43,174 --> 00:30:46,511
our conventional capabilities
on the central front in Europe.

445
00:30:46,511 --> 00:30:49,214
We shifted the balance
between nuclear deterrence

446
00:30:49,214 --> 00:30:52,717
and conventional deterrence
in favor of conventional deterrence.

447
00:30:52,750 --> 00:30:57,655
Number one and number two,
we abandoned the idea that you could fight

448
00:30:57,722 --> 00:31:03,428
a nuclear war with tactical
nuclear weapons in in Europe.

449
00:31:03,428 --> 00:31:09,400
And we instead adopted this policy
to use Thomas Schelling's language.

450
00:31:09,400 --> 00:31:12,737
Would
that might be called manipulation of risk.

451
00:31:12,737 --> 00:31:15,773
We were using a handful of nuclear weapons

452
00:31:15,773 --> 00:31:19,477
to manipulate

453
00:31:19,477 --> 00:31:23,181
the Soviets by by threatening

454
00:31:23,381 --> 00:31:30,154
the possibility of escalation
to the general thermonuclear level.

455
00:31:30,321 --> 00:31:33,157
Now, what does this tell us

456
00:31:33,157 --> 00:31:36,160
about the situation that we're in today?

457
00:31:36,594 --> 00:31:40,398
It tells us that we're in a lot of trouble
because our conventional forces

458
00:31:40,398 --> 00:31:45,904
have been weakened,
and therefore there is powerful incentive

459
00:31:45,904 --> 00:31:48,973
to rely more on nuclear deterrence

460
00:31:48,973 --> 00:31:51,976
than we have on the path in the past,

461
00:31:52,210 --> 00:31:55,513
or at least since the 1950s.

462
00:31:55,880 --> 00:31:58,549
And this is not a good thing.

463
00:31:58,549 --> 00:32:03,354
You want to have a deterrent posture
that relies on conventional

464
00:32:03,354 --> 00:32:07,425
forces and nuclear forces, but you want
the nuclear forces in the background.

465
00:32:07,425 --> 00:32:09,894
You want the conventional forces up front.

466
00:32:09,894 --> 00:32:13,031
But the problem we face,
and this is certainly true in East

467
00:32:13,031 --> 00:32:19,003
Asia today, is that we are in a position
where our conventional forces,

468
00:32:19,003 --> 00:32:23,074
our conventional deterrent,
is nowhere near as formidable

469
00:32:23,074 --> 00:32:28,479
as it should be for a variety of reasons
that are subject for another day.

470
00:32:28,479 --> 00:32:31,482
And therefore we're relying

471
00:32:31,516 --> 00:32:34,319
or thinking about relying more
on nuclear deterrence.

472
00:32:34,319 --> 00:32:38,389
And of course, this has pushed us
to think about tactical nuclear weapons.

473
00:32:38,389 --> 00:32:44,696
But as Carte blanche shows,
this is not a viable strategy.

474
00:32:44,696 --> 00:32:50,668
There's no way the United States
can fight a war with tactical nuclear

475
00:32:50,668 --> 00:32:56,074
weapons in Europe or in East

476
00:32:56,074 --> 00:33:00,812
Asia, and win
that war in any meaningful way.

477
00:33:01,346 --> 00:33:05,984
Carte blanche shows that very clearly,
and nothing has changed since then.

478
00:33:05,984 --> 00:33:10,455
And furthermore,
the threat of escalation is ever present.

479
00:33:10,521 --> 00:33:14,592
So all of this is to say that
what the United States should do

480
00:33:14,625 --> 00:33:18,262
moving forward,
instead of relying on tactical

481
00:33:18,262 --> 00:33:22,600
nuclear weapons
for warfighting purposes, is get back

482
00:33:22,600 --> 00:33:27,138
to the business of building up
its conventional deterrent

483
00:33:27,138 --> 00:33:34,345
and thinking about using nuclear weapons,
if need be, in a much more selective

484
00:33:34,345 --> 00:33:39,951
and coercive way of the kind
that is embodied in MC

485
00:33:40,451 --> 00:33:44,322
14 three, which, as I said,

486
00:33:44,355 --> 00:33:49,060
became NATO doctrine and 1967.

487
00:33:49,360 --> 00:33:51,963
That concludes my episode for today.

488
00:33:51,963 --> 00:33:57,835
I'm quite confident
that there'll be many more episodes down

489
00:33:57,835 --> 00:34:03,341
the road that deal with different aspects
of the nuclear issue, because there's

490
00:34:03,341 --> 00:34:07,745
almost no question that given
the way events are evolving in the world

491
00:34:07,779 --> 00:34:11,382
today, nuclear issues of all sorts

492
00:34:11,682 --> 00:34:16,254
will become much more important
to talk about

493
00:34:16,320 --> 00:34:20,258
than has been the case in recent decades.

494
00:34:20,324 --> 00:34:25,396
Thank you very much for listening,
and I look forward to our next episode.

